  "text": "According to the given information, we have the following equation:\n\n$z^2 - 9 = z(z - 3i)$\n$z^2 - 9 = z^2 - 3iz$\n$|z^2 - 9| = |z(z - 3i)|$\n\nWe know that the modulus of a complex number is always non-negative. Therefore, we can take the modulus of both sides of the equation:\n\n$|z^2 - 9| = |z(z - 3i)|$\n\nApplying the geometric representation of complex numbers on the left and right sides of the equation, we obtain the following figure:\n\n[Image of a geometric figure representing the equation $|z^2 - 9| = |z(z - 3i)|$]\n\nFrom the figure, we can see that the distances from $z$ to $3$ and $2i$ are the same. This implies that $z$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of the line segment connecting $3$ and $2i$. Therefore, the smallest possible value of $|z - 2i|$ takes place when $z = 2i$.\n\nHence, the smallest possible value of $|z - 2i|$ is $\\boxed{1}$.",
